1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,045
March 2019. Eight in the morning.

2
00:00:04,495 --> 00:00:07,241
You hit record, and talk to the person you will become.

3
00:00:07,941 --> 00:00:09,980
Future me, are you still playing guitar?

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:14,484
Over the years, you leave more messages for later.

5
00:00:14,934 --> 00:00:19,819
In June 2021, a prediction: In two years, I will have my own studio.

6
00:00:20,269 --> 00:00:24,503
On New Year's Day, a promise: I promise I will call Dad every Sunday.

7
00:00:24,953 --> 00:00:30,350
And one night in September 2023, a worry: I am scared this job will swallow me.

8
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:32,880
Then life happens.

9
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:37,423
The memos pile up. Two hundred and twelve of them, next to fifty-two notes.

10
00:00:37,873 --> 00:00:39,384
Nobody goes back to listen.

11
00:00:39,834 --> 00:00:43,006
And you can't search for a promise you no longer remember making.

12
00:00:44,356 --> 00:00:46,720
Most archive tools let you ask your past.

13
00:00:47,170 --> 00:00:48,810
This one lets your past ask you.

14
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:51,027
This is Past Me Asks.

15
00:00:51,477 --> 00:00:53,366
A Skill for the UGREEN AI NAS.

16
00:00:54,816 --> 00:00:59,965
It goes through the memos and notes you choose, and finds the messages past you left for the future.

17
00:01:00,415 --> 00:01:03,497
Each one comes back word for word, with its real date.

18
00:01:03,947 --> 00:01:05,564
And then, past you gets to ask.

19
00:01:06,014 --> 00:01:11,189
In 2021, you expected your own studio by 2023. What happened?

20
00:01:12,539 --> 00:01:16,530
You answer in your own words: typed, or recorded as a new Now Me memo.

21
00:01:17,130 --> 00:01:20,431
Not my own, but I share one with Lea since 2024.

22
00:01:21,781 --> 00:01:23,593
Kept? Not kept? Still open?

23
00:01:24,493 --> 00:01:25,505
Only you decide.

24
00:01:25,955 --> 00:01:29,458
It never scores you, never judges you, and never gives advice.

25
00:01:30,908 --> 00:01:32,660
Then the other messages wait for you.

26
00:01:33,110 --> 00:01:34,377
How did this promise go?

27
00:01:34,827 --> 00:01:36,730
What would you tell the person who said this?

28
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:39,003
And that question from 2019.

29
00:01:40,153 --> 00:01:40,712
Are you?

30
00:01:42,012 --> 00:01:46,813
If you are not ready to answer yet, that's fine. It simply saves: not answered yet.

31
00:01:48,513 --> 00:01:53,546
When you say yes, it saves past and present side by side, in one dated dialogue.

32
00:01:53,996 --> 00:01:56,950
Exact quotes. Real dates. Nothing invented.

33
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:01,269
It's made for anyone with years of memos and notes.

34
00:02:01,719 --> 00:02:07,137
Ana turns 35. Past her wanted to have run a marathon by then. She marks it: kept.

35
00:02:07,587 --> 00:02:15,054
Jonas promised to call his dad every Sunday. He writes what happened, and leaves it unmarked. It's the first thing he has written about it.

36
00:02:15,504 --> 00:02:21,974
Sofia answers the worries she had about her teaching job, and the Skill makes no comment on whether they were justified.

37
00:02:22,424 --> 00:02:29,072
And Priya, deciding whether to move abroad again, reads her own words from before, during and after her last move.

38
00:02:30,422 --> 00:02:31,958
And it treats your past with care.

39
00:02:32,408 --> 00:02:38,712
It reads only the folders you name, never changes a memo or a note, and writes nothing until you say yes.

40
00:02:40,662 --> 00:02:43,022
Most archive tools let you ask your past.

41
00:02:43,472 --> 00:02:45,122
This one lets your past ask you.

42
00:02:45,572 --> 00:02:50,246
Past Me Asks. For Bring Memories to Life, by Mert Toprak and Daghan Leflef.

43
00:02:51,746 --> 00:02:54,010
So, tell me. Are you still playing guitar?

